{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until his death in 1187 His reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim states in the region and by the rise of the Zengid dynasty The County of Tripoli was a Crusader state established in the Levant after the First Crusade and Raymond III was its fourth count He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and was born around 1140 Raymond III inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father's death in 1152\n\n*Early reign*\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim states in the region He faced attacks from Nur al-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus In 1157 Raymond III led a campaign against Nur al-Din but was defeated and taken prisoner He was released in 1158 after the intervention of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus\n\nAfter his release Raymond III continued to face challenges from the Muslim states He allied himself with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Edessa in an attempt to counter Nur al-Din's expanding power In 1164 Raymond III was defeated again by Nur al-Din at the Battle of Harim and was once again taken prisoner He was released in 1173 after making a peace agreement with Nur al-Din\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\n\nRaymond III's relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem were complex He was a member of the ruling family of the kingdom and was related to the ruling king Baldwin III through his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem However, Raymond III had a rivalry with Baldwin III's successor Amalric I who accused Raymond III of conspiring with the Muslim states against the Kingdom of Jerusalem In 1174 Raymond III was exiled from the kingdom after being accused of plotting against Amalric I\n\nDespite their rivalry Raymond III and Amalric I reconciled in 1177 when they jointly led an army against Saladin the Ayyubid Sultan of Egypt and Syria Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Montgisard which resulted in a decisive victory for the Crusader forces\n\n*Later reign and death*\n\nRaymond III returned to the County of Tripoli after the Battle of Montgisard and continued to face challenges from the Muslim states He allied himself with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Edessa in an attempt to counter Saladin's expanding power In 1187 Raymond III was killed in the Battle of Hattin which was a devastating defeat for the Crusader forces He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his son Bohemond III\n\nIn conclusion, Raymond III of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until his death in 1187 His reign was marked by conflicts with the Muslim states in the region and by the rise of the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties Raymond III's relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem were complex and he faced accusations of conspiring with the Muslim states against the kingdom Despite these challenges, Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Montgisard which was a decisive victory for the Crusader forces He was killed in the Battle of Hattin which was a devastating defeat for the Crusader forces", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001289761275984347}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.0960646867752075}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -2.169585604860913e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.09771987795829773}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.2047063261270523}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.18540357053279877}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -0.00010895135346800089}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "who", "logprob": -1.0287591218948364}, {"token": "ruled", "logprob": -0.1430466920137